This video is going to be something a little bit different. It's pretty exciting and frankly amazing.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.800
For the longest time, it was thought that brain damage,

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:28.800
such as that caused by a stroke,

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.400
is permanent and irreversible.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.160
But a recent medical breakthrough is bringing this notion into question.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:38.220
Researchers at Stanford University have been blown away by the results

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:42.380
of a clinical trial they conducted. Among other amazing stories,

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.600
a wheelchair-bound stroke victim has regained the ability to walk

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.300
after stem cells were injected directly Into the brain.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:52.420
It's all pretty crazy, but in this video, we'll take a look.

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:59.140
As defined By the US National Institution of Health,

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:04.400
a stroke is a medical condition, in which poor blood flow to the brain results in cell death.

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:08.700
Stanford University tested a new method for recovering damaged brain tissue.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:15.180
The surgical procedure involved 18 stroke patients that were at the Plateau Stage of recovery.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:19.540
At this stage, there's typically no more potential for recovery in motor coordination.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.100
That's pretty much it, that's the best it's gonna get.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.400
They're usually taken out of therapy because it's believed that their brains

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.700
have been damaged in key areas beyond repair.

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:32.280
During the trial, surgeons drilled a hole into the skull of the patients

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.720
and injected human adult stem cells around the damaged areas of the brain.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:41.280
Amazingly, these stem cells then triggered the damaged areas of the brain to repair themselves.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:48.019
Within days, patients could move limbs again, and begin to speak properly when they couldn't previously.

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:50.750
They could even be sent home on the same day as the surgery.

00:01:51.299 --> 00:01:54.739
Here's a news report from CBS on the topic.
It is amazing.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:58.369
We have some remarkable video to show you tonight.

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:05.780
Stroke victims making incredible progress, literally overnight, thanks to a new kind of treatment at Stanford.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:10.720
Five years ago, Sonia Coontz suffered a stroke that severely damaged her brain.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:12.160
This is Sonia's stroke.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.000
It partially paralyzed Sonia on her right side and she could barely speak.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.560
Her speech was not very understandable. She couldn't order food or communicate well.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:26.480
Two years later,  Sonia could still hardly lift her arm, but just one day, after an experimental treatment,

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:27.600
- Oh my gosh.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:32.380
Sonia could lift her arm over her head, and move it to the side.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:33.900
- Are you kidding me?

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:37.820
And also to the front, and her words began to flow.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.060
Sonia: I woke up, and immediately I could speak better

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.160
Dr. Gary Steinberg, Chair of Neurosurgery at Stanford,

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:46.800
led the small clinical trial.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.580
Eighteen chronic stroke patients were involved, twelve came to Stanford, including Sonia.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.940
Sonia: I was very excited. I think I started to cry.

00:02:55.940 --> 00:03:02.000
In a trial, Steinberg drilled a tiny hole Into the patient's skull, and using a very fine needle

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.780
injected modified human adult stem cells around the stroke.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:08.800
You put them around the stroke, and that's where

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:11.200
they do their thing to recover the function.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:13.800
These cells don't survive for long after the surgery,

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.900
but they appear to trigger a patient's damaged brain

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:18.880
to begin to heal Itself.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.240
The cells somehow jumpstart the system again.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:26.200
Here's another stroke patient just before surgery

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:30.340
And here she is just days after receiving the stem-cell treatment.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:36.640
As for Sonia, her life Is back on track. She's now married and pregnant on her first child.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.640
Sonia: And there's a boy. Yeah [laughs]

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:46.600
According to Gary Steinberg, Chairperson of Neurosurgery at Stanford, one of the patients

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:51.840
was a 71 year old wheelchair-bound individual who could walk again after the stem cell surgery.

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:55.040
This event has shocked everyone on the research team.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:56.240
Quote:

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:08.940
He goes on,

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:21.900
End Quote.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.800
So, Gary seems to hint that stem cells could be used

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:26.700
to treat other neuro-degenerative diseases,

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:28.600
like Parkinson's or Alzheimer's.

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:32.980
Alright, so that's all well and good, but let's dig a little deeper:

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:34.980
What exactly are stem cells?

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:39.100
Stem cells are a very remarkable type of cell.

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:44.080
They have the ability to change themselves into any other type of cell during early life and growth.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:48.039
In tissues, they serve as a sort of internal repair system,

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:52.300
dividing essentially without limit to replenish other cells, as long as the person is alive.

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:55.700
They can even do this after long periods of inactivity.

00:04:56.750 --> 00:05:01.200
When a stem cell divides, each new cell has the potential to either remain a stem cell,

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.600
or become another type of cell with a more specialized function,

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:07.029
such as a muscle cell, a red blood cell,

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:09.030
or in this case, a brain cell.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:12.120
In some organs, such as the gut and bone marrow,

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:16.040
stem cells regularly divide to repair and replace worn-out, damaged tissues.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:22.440
In other organs however, such as the pancreas and heart, stem cells only divide under special conditions.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:27.780
The cells used in the Stanford surgery were SB623 stem cells.

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:32.220
These cells are actually a product of SanBio, Inc.

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:37.330
SB623, a bone marrow derived stem cells from adult donors.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:43.000
The new idea here is that these special stem cells have been genetically engineered

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:45.000
to repair damaged cells when introduced.

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:52.800
According to the World Health Organization, 15 million people suffer strokes worldwide each year.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:58.240
Of these, five million die, and another five million are permanently disabled.

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:03.369
Once knowing this, it becomes easy to see the huge potential of this medical breakthrough.

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:11.030
Right now, the Stanford team is now moving Into phase two of the trial, with 153 patients on the list.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:13.940
They're going to see if these results can be replicated.

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:17.220
I'm definitely wishing Gary, and his team, all the best.

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:20.600
I personally think that this is really awesome to see.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:24.200
These are the very early days of stem cell genetic research,

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:26.100
and the technology can only get better from here.

00:06:26.789 --> 00:06:32.200
Clearly, a huge potential lies within stem cell genetic engineering. It definitely can be used for good;

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:34.940
and it's nice to see this technology being used in that way.

